```gherkin
Feature: Checkout Payment Regression — Card Payment with Promo and Stock Validation
  As an e-commerce customer
  I want my card payment authorization and capture to correctly reflect the final cart total, stock availability, and gateway response
  So that I am charged the correct amount and my order status accurately reflects the true outcome of my transaction

  Background:
    Given the customer has an in-stock item added to the cart
    And the customer has selected a saved card as the payment method
    And the payment gateway sandbox environment is available

  @smoke @critical @risk-critical
  Scenario: Authorized amount matches cart total recalculated after promo code application
    Given the customer applies a valid promo code that changes the cart total
    When the customer submits the card payment for authorization
    Then the authorized amount equals the recalculated cart total
    And no amount mismatch is recorded between the cart display and the gateway authorization request

  @regression @risk-critical
  Scenario: Order status is confirmed only after stock reservation and payment capture both succeed
    Given payment capture succeeds for the customer's order
    When stock reservation for the ordered item fails
    Then the order status is not set to confirmed
    And the captured payment is reversed

  @negative @risk-high
  Scenario: Payment capture is blocked when stock is unavailable at capture time
    Given an ordered item's stock quantity has dropped to zero after cart display but before payment capture
    When the customer proceeds to capture payment
    Then the payment capture is blocked
    And the customer is shown a stock-unavailable message

  @regression @data-driven @risk-high
  Scenario Outline: Checkout system handles gateway webhook status and payload per contract
    Given the payment gateway sends a callback with HTTP status code "<status_code>" and payload schema "<payload_type>"
    When the checkout system processes the gateway callback
    Then the checkout system "<handling_result>"

    Examples:
      | status_code | payload_type     | handling_result                                   |
      | 200         | valid_schema     | accepts the callback and updates order status     |
      | 200         | malformed_schema | rejects the payload and logs a schema validation error |
      | 500         | valid_schema     | retries callback processing per contract          |

  @boundary @edge-case @risk-high
  Scenario: Stock validation at capture succeeds when exactly one unit remains
    Given an ordered item's stock quantity is exactly one unit at capture time
    When the customer proceeds to capture payment
    Then the payment capture proceeds
    And the item's stock quantity is decremented to zero

  @security @risk-medium
  Scenario: Card CVV is not exposed in application logs or error responses
    Given the customer submits a card payment that results in a gateway error
    When the error is logged and displayed to the customer
    Then the CVV is not present in the log entry or the error response
    And the card number is displayed only as masked first six and last four digits

  @security @negative @risk-medium
  Scenario: SQL injection payload in promo code field is rejected without data exposure
    Given the customer enters a SQL injection payload into the promo code field
    When the customer submits the checkout form
    Then the system rejects the promo code as invalid
    And no database error or stack trace is exposed to the customer
```

